@node	0.5, 0.6, 0.4, Chapter 0
@section Collections and iterators

Three collection types cover most of what this book needs beyond a bare
@code{struct} or @code{enum}. @code{Vec<T>} is a growable, owned, contiguous
list; @code{&[T]}, a @newterm{slice}, borrows a run of elements from a
@code{Vec} (or an array, or another slice) without owning them, and is almost
always the right parameter type for a function that only reads a sequence.
@code{HashMap<K, V>} maps keys to values with no ordering guarantee, the
book's association lists made efficient.

@example rust
fn word_lengths(text: &str) -> Vec<usize> @{
    text.split_whitespace().map(str::len).collect()
@}

let lengths = word_lengths("ready set go");
println!("@{lengths:?@}");
// => [5, 3, 2]
@end example

@code{split_whitespace} and @code{map} are @newterm{iterator adapters}: each
one describes a step of a walk over the data without doing any work yet.
Nothing runs until something finally consumes the iterator, here
@code{collect}, which is why chaining ten adapters costs no more intermediate
storage than chaining two. This @newterm{laziness} is unlike Scheme's
eagerly-evaluated @code{map}, and closer to the book's own streams
(@ref{3.5}, `` `map`/`filter`/`accumulate` exist twice''): section 3.5
compares the two explicitly once streams are on the table, since a single
@code{Iterator} cursor cannot replace a @code{Stream} everywhere a stream's
sharing matters.

@example rust
use std::collections::HashMap;

fn word_counts<'a>(words: &[&'a str]) -> HashMap<&'a str, usize> @{
    let mut counts = HashMap::new();
    for &word in words @{
        *counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;
    @}
    counts
@}

let words = ["ready", "set", "go", "set"];
let counts = word_counts(&words);
println!("@{@}", counts["set"]);
// => 2
@end example

@code{entry} looks up a key and, in one step, inserts a default if it was
absent; @code{*counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1} reads as ``the count for
@code{word}, starting from zero if this is the first time, plus one.'' This
pattern (`` `entry`, `or_insert`, then mutate'') recurs whenever this book
builds up a table incrementally, including the operation-and-tag tables of
@ref{2.4} and the memoization tables of @ref{3.3.3}.

The runnable listing above is @code{examples/sec_0_5_iterators.rs}.

@subheading A fourth collection: @code{VecDeque}

@code{VecDeque<T>} is a double-ended queue: unlike @code{Vec}, it can push
and pop from @emph{either} end in constant time, at the cost of no longer
guaranteeing its elements sit in one contiguous block of memory.

@example rust
use std::collections::VecDeque;

let mut queue: VecDeque<i32> = VecDeque::new();
queue.push_back(1);
queue.push_back(2);
queue.push_front(0);
println!("@{queue:?@}");
// => [0, 1, 2]

let front = queue.pop_front();
println!("@{front:?@}");
// => Some(0)
@end example

This book reaches for @code{VecDeque} only where a queue's two-ended shape
matters specifically, most visibly chapter 3's message queues (@ref{3.3.2})
and the agenda that drives the digital-circuit simulator (@ref{3.3.4}); a
plain @code{Vec} is the right default everywhere else. The runnable listing
above is @code{examples/sec_0_5_vecdeque.rs}.
